1
00:00:00,235 --> 00:00:03,372
♪ In the
not-too-distant future ♪

2
00:00:03,372 --> 00:00:06,540
♪ Next Sunday, A.D. ♪

3
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:09,577
♪ There was a guy named Joel ♪

4
00:00:09,577 --> 00:00:13,013
♪ Not too different
from you or me ♪

5
00:00:13,013 --> 00:00:15,917
♪ He worked at
Gizmonic Institute ♪

6
00:00:15,917 --> 00:00:19,220
♪ Just another face
in a red jumpsuit ♪

7
00:00:19,220 --> 00:00:22,057
♪ He did a good job
cleaning up the place ♪

8
00:00:22,057 --> 00:00:23,625
♪ But his bosses
didn't like him ♪

9
00:00:23,625 --> 00:00:26,227
♪ So they shot him into space ♪

10
00:00:26,227 --> 00:00:28,530
(rocket ship whirs)

11
00:00:28,530 --> 00:00:31,565
♪ We'll send him cheesy movies ♪

12
00:00:31,565 --> 00:00:34,768
♪ The worst we can find ♪

13
00:00:34,768 --> 00:00:37,705
♪ He'll have to sit
and watch them all ♪

14
00:00:37,705 --> 00:00:41,042
♪ And we'll monitor his mind ♪

15
00:00:41,042 --> 00:00:43,978
♪ Now keep in mind
Joel can't control ♪

16
00:00:43,978 --> 00:00:47,248
♪ Where the movies
begin or end ♪

17
00:00:47,248 --> 00:00:50,285
♪ Because he used
those special parts ♪

18
00:00:50,285 --> 00:00:55,290
♪ To make his robot friends ♪

19
00:00:55,290 --> 00:00:56,892
♪ Robot roll call ♪

20
00:00:56,892 --> 00:00:57,725
♪ Cambot! ♪

21
00:00:57,725 --> 00:00:58,560
♪ Pan left! ♪

22
00:00:58,560 --> 00:00:59,393
♪ Gypsy! ♪

23
00:00:59,393 --> 00:01:00,262
♪ Hi, girl! ♪

24
00:01:00,262 --> 00:01:01,095
♪ Tom Servo ♪

25
00:01:01,095 --> 00:01:01,930
♪ What a cool guy ♪

26
00:01:01,930 --> 00:01:02,763
♪ Crow ♪

27
00:01:02,763 --> 00:01:04,265
♪ He's a wisecracker ♪

28
00:01:04,265 --> 00:01:06,067
♪ If you're wondering how
he eats and breathes ♪

29
00:01:06,067 --> 00:01:09,070
♪ And other science facts ♪

30
00:01:09,070 --> 00:01:10,738
♪ Then repeat to yourself ♪

31
00:01:10,738 --> 00:01:11,738
♪ It's just a show ♪

32
00:01:11,738 --> 00:01:15,277
♪ I should really just relax ♪

33
00:01:15,277 --> 00:01:19,513
♪ For Mystery Science
Theater 3000 ♪

34
00:01:24,285 --> 00:01:26,520
(whirring)

35
00:01:31,025 --> 00:01:35,363
- As the noose was fitted,
and as a delicate black mask

36
00:01:35,363 --> 00:01:37,765
was tied around the
prisoner's eyes,

37
00:01:37,765 --> 00:01:40,100
may the Lord have mercy on--

38
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:42,537
- Eh, boring!

39
00:01:42,537 --> 00:01:44,105
- Boring.
(grumbles)

40
00:01:44,105 --> 00:01:45,940
- Oh hi everybody, welcome
to the satellite of love,

41
00:01:45,940 --> 00:01:48,510
I'm Joel Robinson and
I'm with my robots,

42
00:01:48,510 --> 00:01:50,212
Tom Servo and Crow T. Robot.

43
00:01:50,212 --> 00:01:54,382
And I'm reading them some
really scary bedtime stories.

44
00:01:54,382 --> 00:01:55,850
But they're so jaded, I mean,

45
00:01:55,850 --> 00:01:59,953
kids today have seen and
heard everything, believe me.

46
00:01:59,953 --> 00:02:01,588
- [Robotic voice] Oh,
isn't that the truth?

47
00:02:01,588 --> 00:02:03,758
Commercial sign in 30 seconds.

48
00:02:03,758 --> 00:02:05,493
- C'mon, Joel, you've
been reading us

49
00:02:05,493 --> 00:02:06,727
nothing but the light stuff!

50
00:02:06,727 --> 00:02:08,697
"In Cold Blood,"
"Helter-Skelter,"

51
00:02:08,697 --> 00:02:11,398
the 17 novels that Stephen
King published this year.

52
00:02:11,398 --> 00:02:12,400
Come on!

53
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:14,135
Read us something really scary.

54
00:02:14,135 --> 00:02:15,537
- Yeah, come on.
- Okay,

55
00:02:15,537 --> 00:02:19,173
I've been saving a
really, really scary one.

56
00:02:19,173 --> 00:02:21,208
That is, if you guys
think you're old enough,

57
00:02:21,208 --> 00:02:22,810
if you think you can handle it.

58
00:02:22,810 --> 00:02:25,447
- Oh, I'm sure
it's really scary.

59
00:02:25,447 --> 00:02:27,882
Do I dare ask what it's called?

60
00:02:27,882 --> 00:02:29,450
- "Life's Little
Instruction Book."

61
00:02:29,450 --> 00:02:30,285
(screams)

62
00:02:30,285 --> 00:02:31,085
- Oh no!

63
00:02:31,085 --> 00:02:32,453
- Okay, number one.

64
00:02:32,453 --> 00:02:35,055
Put a lot of little marshmallows
in your hot chocolate.

65
00:02:35,055 --> 00:02:35,890
(both scream)

66
00:02:35,890 --> 00:02:37,158
Number two.

67
00:02:37,158 --> 00:02:38,527
Surprise a new neighbor

68
00:02:38,527 --> 00:02:40,262
with one of your
favorite homemade dishes.

69
00:02:40,262 --> 00:02:41,095
(gasp)

70
00:02:41,095 --> 00:02:41,930
And include the recipe.

71
00:02:41,930 --> 00:02:43,565
- Not the recipe!

72
00:02:43,565 --> 00:02:44,665
- Number three.

73
00:02:44,665 --> 00:02:46,567
At the movies, buy Junior Mints

74
00:02:46,567 --> 00:02:48,603
and sprinkle them
on your popcorn!

75
00:02:48,603 --> 00:02:49,903
(garbled yells)

76
00:02:49,903 --> 00:02:53,373
Number four, enjoy
real maple syrup.

77
00:02:53,373 --> 00:02:55,943
- The humor, the humor!

78
00:02:55,943 --> 00:02:59,080
(upbeat synth music)

79
00:03:02,183 --> 00:03:03,885
- Number 187.

80
00:03:03,885 --> 00:03:06,087
Measure people by the
size of their hearts,

81
00:03:06,087 --> 00:03:08,155
not by the size of
their bank accounts.

82
00:03:08,155 --> 00:03:09,390
- Ugh, that's enough, Joel,

83
00:03:09,390 --> 00:03:11,692
I can't take any
more, please, no!

84
00:03:11,692 --> 00:03:13,193
- Keep going, keep going.

85
00:03:13,193 --> 00:03:14,728
- What are you, crazy?
- Okay.

86
00:03:14,728 --> 00:03:15,663
Number 188.

87
00:03:15,663 --> 00:03:16,898
When facing a difficult task,

88
00:03:16,898 --> 00:03:19,067
act as though it's
impossible to fail.

89
00:03:19,067 --> 00:03:22,770
When going after Moby Dick,
take along the tartar sauce.

90
00:03:22,770 --> 00:03:25,507
(both scream)

91
00:03:25,507 --> 00:03:28,542
Hold the phone, you two, Mum
Mum and Pee Pop are calling.

92
00:03:28,542 --> 00:03:30,077
- That's nothing, Joel.

93
00:03:30,077 --> 00:03:32,647
Clive Barker says, "I have
seen the future of horror

94
00:03:32,647 --> 00:03:34,782
"and it's 'Everything
I Need to Know,

95
00:03:34,782 --> 00:03:36,417
"I Learned in Kindergarten.'"

96
00:03:36,417 --> 00:03:38,620
(evil laugh)

97
00:03:38,620 --> 00:03:41,088
- Ready with the invention
exchange, Chiefirino.

98
00:03:41,088 --> 00:03:42,923
- Each day, Frank and I
are consumed with many

99
00:03:42,923 --> 00:03:44,458
time constraints,

100
00:03:44,458 --> 00:03:46,693
what with the mountain of
paperwork here at the lab,

101
00:03:46,693 --> 00:03:49,330
Frank's social calendar,
and my jazzercise,

102
00:03:49,330 --> 00:03:51,765
we just don't have
time to relax.

103
00:03:51,765 --> 00:03:54,068
At the odd occasion we
do have an evening free

104
00:03:54,068 --> 00:03:56,103
to take in, say, a film,

105
00:03:56,103 --> 00:03:57,805
we run around here like ninnies,

106
00:03:57,805 --> 00:04:00,007
trying to shower and
shave and get there

107
00:04:00,007 --> 00:04:01,808
only to spend a
half hour in line.

108
00:04:01,808 --> 00:04:04,545
- So do what I do.

109
00:04:04,545 --> 00:04:06,913
Don't get ready to go
until you're already there!

110
00:04:06,913 --> 00:04:07,748
How?

111
00:04:07,748 --> 00:04:08,883
It's easy!

112
00:04:08,883 --> 00:04:11,987
With the new Frank-brand
Quik-Primp kit.

113
00:04:12,953 --> 00:04:14,455
- That's right.

114
00:04:14,455 --> 00:04:16,923
Spend that down time in line
doing something productive

115
00:04:16,923 --> 00:04:20,795
like teasing your brow, or
finding your natural part.

116
00:04:20,795 --> 00:04:22,697
Now, say, for
demonstration purposes

117
00:04:22,697 --> 00:04:25,533
Frank and I are in
line to see, oh,

118
00:04:25,533 --> 00:04:26,567
"Out On a Limb."

119
00:04:27,702 --> 00:04:29,403
- Well in that case,
we'd be seated already.

120
00:04:29,403 --> 00:04:30,438
- Frank.

121
00:04:30,438 --> 00:04:32,740
- Okay, just for
argument's sake,

122
00:04:32,740 --> 00:04:37,012
let's say we're on line
to see "Out On a Limb."

123
00:04:38,245 --> 00:04:39,180
Anyway.

124
00:04:39,180 --> 00:04:40,982
Now I'm not nearly ready to go.

125
00:04:40,982 --> 00:04:42,250
But it doesn't matter.

126
00:04:42,250 --> 00:04:45,687
I merely take this
light and portable

127
00:04:46,820 --> 00:04:49,290
Frank-brand full-length mirror.

128
00:04:49,290 --> 00:04:52,893
Then I quickly secure
the whisper-weight

129
00:04:52,893 --> 00:04:56,830
Frank-brand toiletry chapeau.

130
00:04:56,830 --> 00:04:58,032
(groans)

131
00:04:58,032 --> 00:05:00,133
Really kind of depends
on the good graces

132
00:05:00,133 --> 00:05:02,403
of the person standing
in front of you.

133
00:05:02,403 --> 00:05:06,173
I find average height
really works best.

134
00:05:06,173 --> 00:05:07,108
(laughs)

135
00:05:07,108 --> 00:05:08,142
I'll be done in just a...

136
00:05:08,142 --> 00:05:09,043
(laughs)

137
00:05:09,043 --> 00:05:11,012
Do I look fat to you?

138
00:05:11,012 --> 00:05:12,080
Just a second.

139
00:05:13,480 --> 00:05:14,315
Hey.

140
00:05:14,315 --> 00:05:15,950
(laughs)

141
00:05:15,950 --> 00:05:17,785
Back up to you, Mr. R.

142
00:05:17,785 --> 00:05:19,720
- Well our invention
exchange this week

143
00:05:19,720 --> 00:05:22,057
takes us to the world of art.

144
00:05:22,057 --> 00:05:24,492
- Abstract expressionism,
it's what's hot!

145
00:05:24,492 --> 00:05:26,660
All the kids are dying for it.

146
00:05:26,660 --> 00:05:28,128
(laughs)

147
00:05:28,128 --> 00:05:29,597
- That's why we did the
paint-by-number Mark Rothko.

148
00:05:29,597 --> 00:05:31,998
Now even little Johnny can
join the color-filled painters

149
00:05:31,998 --> 00:05:33,902
and challenge the
convention of the art world!

150
00:05:33,902 --> 00:05:35,370
- Yeah, let's see here.

151
00:05:35,370 --> 00:05:37,605
We got our Number Five paint,
it says number five here.

152
00:05:37,605 --> 00:05:41,575
We just paint the brush
and start painting.

153
00:05:41,575 --> 00:05:42,577
(both stammer)

154
00:05:42,577 --> 00:05:43,812
- It's fun!
- Uh, Joel,

155
00:05:43,812 --> 00:05:45,145
are you sure you're
doing that right?

156
00:05:45,145 --> 00:05:46,613
Let's take a look
at the box, eh?

157
00:05:46,613 --> 00:05:48,015
- Yeah, let's see what.

158
00:05:48,015 --> 00:05:48,950
Oh, what do you know?

159
00:05:48,950 --> 00:05:50,217
- Looks pretty good to me.

160
00:05:50,217 --> 00:05:51,685
- Whaddaya think, sirs?

161
00:05:51,685 --> 00:05:54,155
- I don't know much about
art, but I know what I like.

162
00:05:54,155 --> 00:05:55,790
And I like today's film.

163
00:05:55,790 --> 00:05:58,192
You young Turks are in
for the wackiest ride

164
00:05:58,192 --> 00:06:00,928
since Richard Pryor
boarded the Silver Streak.

165
00:06:00,928 --> 00:06:03,363
Please enjoy "The Rebel Set."

166
00:06:03,363 --> 00:06:06,100
But first, an aptly-named film

167
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:08,302
entitled "Johnny at the Fair."

168
00:06:09,837 --> 00:06:11,005
- More like that?

169
00:06:11,005 --> 00:06:12,107
- No no no no,
right at the center.

170
00:06:12,107 --> 00:06:13,842
- Like down?
- Down, the other way.

171
00:06:13,842 --> 00:06:15,342
- That's fine.
- What do you want?

172
00:06:15,342 --> 00:06:20,047
- Holy then movie starts!
(screams)

173
00:06:20,047 --> 00:06:23,050
(doors whir, clank)

174
00:06:27,053 --> 00:06:28,288
- [Joel] Come on you
guys, now this is a short.

175
00:06:28,288 --> 00:06:29,923
I don't want us
getting too dark.

176
00:06:29,923 --> 00:06:31,525
- [Crow] Okay, okay.

177
00:06:31,525 --> 00:06:34,195
- [Joel] I know, it's a new
band from Seattle, right?

178
00:06:35,997 --> 00:06:37,598
- [Crow] I'm gonna
get Joe Louis.

179
00:06:37,598 --> 00:06:38,565
- [Servo] Oh, Barbara Ann Scott,

180
00:06:38,565 --> 00:06:40,768
the white bomber, I suppose?

181
00:06:40,768 --> 00:06:42,870
- [Crow] Olsen and
Johnson, keeps babies soft.

182
00:06:42,870 --> 00:06:45,973
- [Joel] And Johnny, the
Wonder Something-or-other.

183
00:06:45,973 --> 00:06:48,508
- [Servo] Look, it's
the Arc de Full Retreat.

184
00:06:48,508 --> 00:06:51,678
- [Crow] Prozac, get
work in today's society.

185
00:06:51,678 --> 00:06:52,713
- [Voiceover] This is
the story of Johnny.

186
00:06:52,713 --> 00:06:53,948
- [Joel] A sad story.

187
00:06:53,948 --> 00:06:55,450
- [Narrator] It all
happened the day Johnny

188
00:06:55,450 --> 00:06:58,485
and his mother and
dad crossed the border

189
00:06:58,485 --> 00:07:00,287
to see the famous Canadian
National Exhibition.

190
00:07:00,287 --> 00:07:01,788
- [Servo] Oh, that sounds great.

191
00:07:01,788 --> 00:07:03,623
- [Narrator] It was Johnny's
first real grown-up trip,

192
00:07:03,623 --> 00:07:05,693
and there were
many things to see.

193
00:07:05,693 --> 00:07:08,897
- [Crow] This is the last
you'll see Johnny happy.

194
00:07:08,897 --> 00:07:10,798
- [Narrator] There
were exhibits on radio,

195
00:07:10,798 --> 00:07:13,500
electricity,
flowers, photography,

196
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:15,737
a real land of wonders.

197
00:07:15,737 --> 00:07:16,970
- [Robots] Boring.

198
00:07:16,970 --> 00:07:18,305
- [Narrator] And a
whole Scottish band.

199
00:07:19,440 --> 00:07:21,942
Johnny even got to
the midway for a ride,

200
00:07:21,942 --> 00:07:23,812
but the fun didn't last
nearly long enough.

201
00:07:23,812 --> 00:07:25,212
- [Servo] Johnny's
car rolled and burned.

202
00:07:25,212 --> 00:07:27,182
- [Narrator] There were displays
from all over the world.

203
00:07:27,182 --> 00:07:29,917
From countries Johnny
was just learning about.

204
00:07:29,917 --> 00:07:31,318
Fine porcelains from France.

205
00:07:32,187 --> 00:07:34,088
Riches from the Orient.

206
00:07:34,088 --> 00:07:35,623
Silks and pearls from India.

207
00:07:35,623 --> 00:07:37,892
- [Joel] Simulated
culture like Disney World.

208
00:07:39,093 --> 00:07:40,662
- [Narrator] It had
been a busy morning,

209
00:07:40,662 --> 00:07:44,398
but Johnny's adventure really
began before lunchtime.

210
00:07:44,398 --> 00:07:45,900
- [Crow] A shot rang out!

211
00:07:45,900 --> 00:07:46,833
- [Narrator] Everyone was
having a good time but it's

212
00:07:46,833 --> 00:07:48,368
hard to decide where to go next.

213
00:07:48,368 --> 00:07:50,437
- [Joel] How 'bout home?

214
00:07:50,437 --> 00:07:52,240
- [Narrator] "Let's go back
to the midway," he says,

215
00:07:52,240 --> 00:07:54,175
pointing in the wrong direction.

216
00:07:54,175 --> 00:07:55,643
"What Johnny?

217
00:07:55,643 --> 00:07:57,612
"No, Johnny" says mom, "we're
going to the art gallery."

218
00:07:57,612 --> 00:07:59,213
- [Servo] And you'll like it!

219
00:07:59,213 --> 00:08:00,915
- [Crow] (imitating petulant
child) No I don't wanna go!

220
00:08:01,848 --> 00:08:03,117
- [Narrator] That's no fun.

221
00:08:03,117 --> 00:08:05,085
Let's see what a fella
can find for himself.

222
00:08:05,085 --> 00:08:08,488
- [Joel] Johnny's negligent
parents let him wander off.

223
00:08:08,488 --> 00:08:10,057
- [Servo] (imitating child)
Haight Ashbury, please?

224
00:08:12,427 --> 00:08:13,995
Ding ding ding.

225
00:08:13,995 --> 00:08:16,163
- [Narrator] Johnny's
parents still can't decide.

226
00:08:16,163 --> 00:08:17,965
At first, they don't miss him.

227
00:08:17,965 --> 00:08:18,798
- [Crow] Who, what?

228
00:08:18,798 --> 00:08:19,733
- [Narrator] Then.

229
00:08:19,733 --> 00:08:20,668
- [Servo] A feeling of elation.

230
00:08:20,668 --> 00:08:22,068
- [Narrator] Where's Johnny?

231
00:08:22,068 --> 00:08:23,803
Where did that boy go?

232
00:08:23,803 --> 00:08:27,842
With 250,000 people here,
how will we ever find him?

233
00:08:27,842 --> 00:08:30,210
- [Servo] Johnny's parents
decide to move on and start

234
00:08:30,210 --> 00:08:31,412
their lives anew.

235
00:08:31,412 --> 00:08:32,847
- [Narrator] "Ah,"
thinks Johnny.

236
00:08:32,847 --> 00:08:34,348
- [Crow] (imitating
child) Ah, sweet freedom.

237
00:08:34,348 --> 00:08:36,317
- [Narrator] With 15 cents in
his pocket and an afternoon

238
00:08:36,317 --> 00:08:39,053
ahead of him, Johnny puts
his best foot forward.

239
00:08:39,053 --> 00:08:41,822
He isn't sure where he's
going, but he's on his way.

240
00:08:41,822 --> 00:08:43,390
- [Servo] Girls!

241
00:08:44,992 --> 00:08:46,293
- [Crow] Run, John, run!

242
00:08:46,293 --> 00:08:47,127
- [Narrator] Then Johnny
hears a sound that

243
00:08:47,127 --> 00:08:48,562
starts him running.

244
00:08:48,562 --> 00:08:49,597
A baseball game.

245
00:08:49,597 --> 00:08:51,398
Oh, boy!

246
00:08:51,398 --> 00:08:54,135
When he gets there, he
finds a five year old can't

247
00:08:54,135 --> 00:08:55,703
get close enough
to see anything.

248
00:08:55,703 --> 00:08:58,072
- [Servo] Besides, the Mariners
are playing, so who cares?

249
00:08:58,072 --> 00:08:59,773
- [Narrator] Unless someone
lifts you up and puts

250
00:08:59,773 --> 00:09:02,843
you on the lap of the undefeated
champion of the world,

251
00:09:02,843 --> 00:09:04,078
Joe Louis.

252
00:09:04,078 --> 00:09:06,047
- [Crow] Joe hits Johnny
up for 15 cents due to

253
00:09:06,047 --> 00:09:06,880
tax problems.

254
00:09:06,880 --> 00:09:08,950
(groans)

255
00:09:10,685 --> 00:09:13,887
- [Joel] Half the pain of
having feet is Red Goose shoes.

256
00:09:13,887 --> 00:09:16,123
- [Narrator] Next
stop, the horse show.

257
00:09:16,123 --> 00:09:17,825
Johnny loves horses.

258
00:09:17,825 --> 00:09:19,193
He's just in time,

259
00:09:19,193 --> 00:09:21,962
for the judges are about
to award the prize.

260
00:09:21,962 --> 00:09:23,663
- [Servo] And it goes to Johnny!

261
00:09:23,663 --> 00:09:24,898
(chuckles)

262
00:09:24,898 --> 00:09:26,733
- [Narrator] The winner
is this white beauty.

263
00:09:26,733 --> 00:09:28,602
- [Crow] (imitating
horse) Oh, my ear!

264
00:09:28,602 --> 00:09:29,437
Ow!

265
00:09:31,138 --> 00:09:34,508
Geez, the horse from Equus
had it better than this.

266
00:09:34,508 --> 00:09:37,912
- [Servo] Then Johnny
transmogrifies, he's
a shape shifter.

267
00:09:37,912 --> 00:09:40,013
And he breaks the fourth seal.

268
00:09:40,013 --> 00:09:41,215
- [Narrator] It's a
five day old Shetland.

269
00:09:41,215 --> 00:09:43,750
And was born right at the fair.

270
00:09:43,750 --> 00:09:47,855
He weighs all of 24 pounds and
is just under two feet tall.

271
00:09:47,855 --> 00:09:50,625
- [Joel] His parents didn't
abandon him like Johnny's.

272
00:09:50,625 --> 00:09:52,258
(suckles)

273
00:09:52,258 --> 00:09:53,427
- [Crow] Oops, sorry dad.

274
00:09:54,862 --> 00:09:57,532
- [Servo] Johnny feels dark
hands pressing him onward.

275
00:09:57,532 --> 00:09:59,967
The voices in his
head get meaner.

276
00:09:59,967 --> 00:10:01,368
- [Narrator] Johnny
can't read the words

277
00:10:01,368 --> 00:10:02,603
"Chemical Wonderland."

278
00:10:02,603 --> 00:10:04,372
- [Joel] Oh, we've
all been there.

279
00:10:04,372 --> 00:10:06,740
- [Narrator] He thinks
he'll check on things.

280
00:10:06,740 --> 00:10:08,775
- [Crow] A whiskery man
hands him a small package,

281
00:10:08,775 --> 00:10:11,045
"The first one's free," he says.

282
00:10:11,045 --> 00:10:13,347
- [Narrator] In a little
vat, and low and behold

283
00:10:13,347 --> 00:10:15,015
before his very eyes.

284
00:10:15,015 --> 00:10:15,850
- [Servo] LSD.

285
00:10:16,917 --> 00:10:18,085
- [Narrator] A rubber ball.

286
00:10:18,085 --> 00:10:19,020
- [Servo] (disappointed) Oh.

287
00:10:19,020 --> 00:10:20,520
- [Narrator] Thank you!

288
00:10:20,520 --> 00:10:22,723
- [Joel] Johnny must have
everything for himself.

289
00:10:22,723 --> 00:10:24,792
- [Narrator] Johnny's
off for more adventures.

290
00:10:27,795 --> 00:10:30,163
Gee whiz, I'll be they don't
have anything like this

291
00:10:30,163 --> 00:10:31,865
in the art gallery.

292
00:10:31,865 --> 00:10:33,733
- [Crow] Johnny distracts
the tightrope walkers

293
00:10:33,733 --> 00:10:35,937
and they fall to certain doom.

294
00:10:35,937 --> 00:10:38,138
- [Narrator] And
on bicycles, too.

295
00:10:38,138 --> 00:10:39,940
- [Joel] Boy, they're sure
tough on drunk drivers

296
00:10:39,940 --> 00:10:40,742
in Canada.

297
00:10:46,113 --> 00:10:48,948
- [Narrator] Johnny is invited
to help put on the show.

298
00:10:48,948 --> 00:10:51,218
And don't think he
doesn't enjoy himself.

299
00:10:51,218 --> 00:10:52,285
- [Joel] That's a guy!

300
00:10:53,753 --> 00:10:55,522
- [Narrator] Afterwards,
Johnny can't stop going up

301
00:10:55,522 --> 00:10:56,823
and down.

302
00:10:56,823 --> 00:10:57,658
- [Crow] The drugs from
the chemical wonderland

303
00:10:57,658 --> 00:10:58,525
start to kick in.

304
00:11:00,895 --> 00:11:02,363
(chuckles)

305
00:11:02,363 --> 00:11:03,530
- [Narrator] Johnny's mother
is still trying to find

306
00:11:03,530 --> 00:11:04,932
her missing son,

307
00:11:04,932 --> 00:11:07,167
and thinks she might see
Johnny on the edge of the crowd

308
00:11:07,167 --> 00:11:08,768
gathered to hear the
former Prime Minister.

309
00:11:08,768 --> 00:11:09,903
- [Joel] Yeah, right.

310
00:11:09,903 --> 00:11:11,838
- [Narrator] Mr. Mackenzie King.

311
00:11:11,838 --> 00:11:14,475
She never dreams that
Johnny is on the platform,

312
00:11:14,475 --> 00:11:16,877
discussing the situation
with the guest speaker.

313
00:11:16,877 --> 00:11:18,512
- [Servo] Tell me, Johnny,
what do you think of the new

314
00:11:18,512 --> 00:11:20,047
free trade agreement?

315
00:11:20,047 --> 00:11:22,850
- [Crow] I have to go horsies.

316
00:11:22,850 --> 00:11:24,118
- [Joel] What was
his name again?

317
00:11:24,118 --> 00:11:26,453
Jimmy, no Jerry?

318
00:11:26,453 --> 00:11:28,988
- [Narrator] Johnny gets an
escort down from the platform

319
00:11:28,988 --> 00:11:30,390
and is off again.

320
00:11:30,390 --> 00:11:31,992
- [Crow] It will be the
first of many encounters

321
00:11:31,992 --> 00:11:33,293
with bouncers for Johnny.

322
00:11:34,195 --> 00:11:36,697
(Servo honks)

323
00:11:38,865 --> 00:11:41,602
- [Narrator] Oh, boy,
a helicopter airplane.

324
00:11:41,602 --> 00:11:42,737
- [Servo] What?

325
00:11:42,737 --> 00:11:44,037
- [Narrator] "Jimminy,"
thinks Johnny.

326
00:11:44,037 --> 00:11:45,705
"If only I could get a
ride in one of those."

327
00:11:45,705 --> 00:11:47,742
- [Servo] Visions of the
Mekong Delta flash before

328
00:11:47,742 --> 00:11:48,642
Johnny's eyes.

329
00:11:51,778 --> 00:11:53,313
- [Narrator] What a day.

330
00:11:53,313 --> 00:11:55,015
You never know who a fella
will meet when he's looking

331
00:11:55,015 --> 00:11:56,283
for a helicopter.

332
00:11:56,283 --> 00:11:58,185
It's Barbara Anne Scott.

333
00:11:58,185 --> 00:11:59,387
(purrs)

334
00:11:59,387 --> 00:12:00,653
The world's champion
figure skater.

335
00:12:00,653 --> 00:12:02,088
- [Joel] She's the
Cher of her time.

336
00:12:02,088 --> 00:12:03,623
- [Crow] "Ooh,
jimminy," thinks Johhny,

337
00:12:03,623 --> 00:12:06,093
"if only I could get a
ride in one of those."

338
00:12:09,497 --> 00:12:12,165
- [Narrator] Johnny does
find a real airplane,

339
00:12:12,165 --> 00:12:13,667
and gets his ride.

340
00:12:13,667 --> 00:12:15,302
- [Servo] Johnny thinks
Amelia Earhart seems like

341
00:12:15,302 --> 00:12:16,537
a nice lady.

342
00:12:16,537 --> 00:12:18,005
- [Narrator] They take
a spin on Lake Ontario,

343
00:12:18,005 --> 00:12:20,440
to watch the start of the
International Speed Boat Race.

344
00:12:20,440 --> 00:12:23,910
- [Servo] Whoa, Johnny
realizes he's in over his head.

345
00:12:25,578 --> 00:12:27,748
- [Crow] Johnny is irresponsibly
put into a dangerous

346
00:12:27,748 --> 00:12:29,517
speedboat without a life jacket.

347
00:12:29,517 --> 00:12:30,785
- [Servo] For the first
time in his young life,

348
00:12:30,785 --> 00:12:32,887
Johnny knows real fear.

349
00:12:32,887 --> 00:12:34,322
(imitates screaming)

350
00:12:34,322 --> 00:12:36,523
- [Joel] Johnny's hydro-plane
disintegrates on impact.

351
00:12:40,260 --> 00:12:42,830
- [Crow] Johnny's spirit
is completely broken.

352
00:12:42,830 --> 00:12:43,963
- [Narrator] By now the
spring in Johnny's step

353
00:12:43,963 --> 00:12:45,698
is nearly gone.

354
00:12:45,698 --> 00:12:47,568
- [Servo] (singing)
Boy for sale,

355
00:12:49,002 --> 00:12:50,370
he's going cheap!

356
00:12:52,438 --> 00:12:54,775
- [Narrator] There's just
so much a guy can take.

357
00:12:54,775 --> 00:12:57,512
- [Servo] Johnny realizes
what a lie his life has been.

358
00:13:00,413 --> 00:13:02,983
- [Narrator] Right now,
Johnny wants his mother.

359
00:13:02,983 --> 00:13:05,785
Perhaps Olsen and Johnson,
the Hellzapopin boys

360
00:13:05,785 --> 00:13:07,053
can cheer Johnny up.

361
00:13:07,053 --> 00:13:08,155
- [Crow] Yeah, but if
you've seen their act,

362
00:13:08,155 --> 00:13:09,490
you know that's not likely.

363
00:13:11,058 --> 00:13:12,793
- [Narrator] Johnny
wants no part of them.

364
00:13:12,793 --> 00:13:14,027
- [Joel] Johnny's got taste.

365
00:13:14,027 --> 00:13:15,628
- [Narrator] But there's
tricks to every trade.

366
00:13:15,628 --> 00:13:17,330
And the Hellzapopin
boys are tops.

367
00:13:18,732 --> 00:13:21,768
Even a very tired and hungry
Johnny is able to laugh.

368
00:13:21,768 --> 00:13:23,503
- [Servo] Laugh, laugh,
it's funny, laugh!

369
00:13:26,107 --> 00:13:28,642
- [Narrator] Finally, a
friendly policeman takes Johnny

370
00:13:28,642 --> 00:13:31,312
to a building he hadn't
planned on seeing.

371
00:13:33,880 --> 00:13:35,348
- [Joel] (imitating
child) Let me out!

372
00:13:35,348 --> 00:13:36,583
Attica, Attica!

373
00:13:39,820 --> 00:13:41,655
- [Narrator] There are
350 acres of ground in

374
00:13:41,655 --> 00:13:43,090
Exhibition Park.

375
00:13:43,090 --> 00:13:45,592
And mom and pop have
covered every single acre

376
00:13:45,592 --> 00:13:46,793
looking for Johnny.

377
00:13:47,928 --> 00:13:49,162
- [Servo] Did I tell
you to have this baby?!

378
00:13:49,162 --> 00:13:50,230
- [Narrator] They've
looked everywhere.

379
00:13:50,230 --> 00:13:51,998
Everywhere but one place.

380
00:13:51,998 --> 00:13:53,367
- [Crow] Their own hearts.

381
00:13:56,003 --> 00:13:57,437
- [Narrator] He's about so high.

382
00:13:57,437 --> 00:13:58,272
- [Servo] (imitating
Woman) Well, yes, there was

383
00:13:58,272 --> 00:13:59,340
a Mr. Barty here.

384
00:14:05,578 --> 00:14:07,615
(all clap)

385
00:14:07,615 --> 00:14:10,617
- [Servo] Mommy had a little
vacation while you were lost.

386
00:14:10,617 --> 00:14:12,687
- [Joel] Yeah, right,
play it up big in public,

387
00:14:12,687 --> 00:14:13,587
that's right.

388
00:14:17,858 --> 00:14:18,958
- [Crow] (imitating child)
And I met Joe Louis,

389
00:14:18,958 --> 00:14:20,193
and the Prime Minister.

390
00:14:20,193 --> 00:14:21,628
- [Joel] Yeah, whatever.

391
00:14:21,628 --> 00:14:24,130
- [Narrator] Johnny fell asleep
on his father's shoulder,

392
00:14:24,130 --> 00:14:26,800
he had managed to stay awake
just long enough to see

393
00:14:26,800 --> 00:14:28,768
the beginning of the fireworks.

394
00:14:28,768 --> 00:14:30,270
- [Joel] At home.

395
00:14:30,270 --> 00:14:31,705
- [Crow] Johnny's parents
blamed each other and never

396
00:14:31,705 --> 00:14:33,240
trusted again.

397
00:14:33,240 --> 00:14:34,073
Goodnight.

398
00:14:35,175 --> 00:14:36,843
- [Narrator] Pleasant
dreams, Johnny.

399
00:14:36,843 --> 00:14:38,478
- [Servo] So long, sucker.

400
00:14:39,647 --> 00:14:41,582
- [Crow] Let this be
a warning, to you.

401
00:14:42,683 --> 00:14:45,285
(upbeat music)

402
00:14:48,922 --> 00:14:50,223
(bass)

403
00:14:50,223 --> 00:14:52,058
Hey, there's a Mingus among us.

404
00:14:52,058 --> 00:14:53,327
(chuckling)

405
00:14:53,327 --> 00:14:54,562
- [Joel] Oh, you know I
got the nicest rebel set

406
00:14:54,562 --> 00:14:55,762
for my wedding, it's so nice.

407
00:14:55,762 --> 00:14:58,665
Oh, yeah, it's reasonable.

408
00:15:01,068 --> 00:15:03,137
- [Crow] Yusef Lateef,
I-sef Lateef, let's call the

409
00:15:03,137 --> 00:15:04,705
whole thing off.

410
00:15:04,705 --> 00:15:07,808
- [Servo] Man, I wish I
could afford a trap set man.

411
00:15:08,875 --> 00:15:09,710
Man.

412
00:15:10,843 --> 00:15:13,113
- [Joel] Oh, I never
go down to the Village,

413
00:15:13,113 --> 00:15:14,448
they're too nutty down there.

414
00:15:14,448 --> 00:15:16,617
- [Robots] Oh, yeah, oh sure.

415
00:15:16,617 --> 00:15:17,618
- [Servo] Wigged out.

416
00:15:19,653 --> 00:15:22,288
- [Crow] Looking very relaxed,
Adolf Hitler on vibes.

417
00:15:27,160 --> 00:15:29,397
- [Joel] (imitating woman)
Cigar, cigarettes, Camus,

418
00:15:29,397 --> 00:15:32,900
Sartre, Ernst,
alienation, Vitchenstein.

419
00:15:32,900 --> 00:15:34,535
(exclaim)

420
00:15:34,535 --> 00:15:35,868
Wow, close, that was close.

421
00:15:40,407 --> 00:15:42,677
(scatting)

422
00:15:44,210 --> 00:15:46,647
- [Crow] Hey, it's
Fisher and Spasky.

423
00:15:46,647 --> 00:15:48,415
- [Servo] (singing) One night
in Bangkok makes a strong

424
00:15:48,415 --> 00:15:49,248
man crumble!

425
00:15:50,383 --> 00:15:52,085
- [Joel] Boy, those
rebels really go through

426
00:15:52,085 --> 00:15:53,053
the Swiss Miss.

427
00:15:55,222 --> 00:15:56,657
- [Crow] You know this is
the hippest Red Lobster

428
00:15:56,657 --> 00:15:57,490
I've been to.

429
00:15:59,092 --> 00:16:00,760
- [Servo] Ah shit,
here she comes again.

430
00:16:00,760 --> 00:16:02,663
(sigh)

431
00:16:03,897 --> 00:16:06,167
(scatting)

432
00:16:08,002 --> 00:16:09,202
- [Joel] Oh, waitress?

433
00:16:09,202 --> 00:16:11,938
Um, excuse me,
could we get some,

434
00:16:11,938 --> 00:16:12,773
(whistles)

435
00:16:12,773 --> 00:16:13,607
Uh.

436
00:16:13,607 --> 00:16:14,442
(whistles)

437
00:16:14,442 --> 00:16:15,275
Forget it.

438
00:16:16,977 --> 00:16:19,513
(scatting)

439
00:16:19,513 --> 00:16:23,417
- Spraying perfumes of
France upon this beat.

440
00:16:24,317 --> 00:16:26,020
Fleeing atoms of rose,

441
00:16:26,020 --> 00:16:27,153
(snoring)

442
00:16:27,153 --> 00:16:28,388
upon this pair.

443
00:16:28,388 --> 00:16:30,723
Stew oceans of lotions,

444
00:16:30,723 --> 00:16:32,558
and liquid commotions.

445
00:16:33,727 --> 00:16:37,030
Put cologne on the
beards of each lamb.

446
00:16:37,030 --> 00:16:38,465
- [Crow] Oh, that's profound.

447
00:16:38,465 --> 00:16:39,800
Check please.

448
00:16:39,800 --> 00:16:40,733
- Oh, King.

449
00:16:42,535 --> 00:16:44,037
- Hey!

450
00:16:44,037 --> 00:16:45,172
- [Crow] Hey, what do you
want, I'm a character actor!

451
00:16:45,172 --> 00:16:46,340
- Good evening, Mr. T.

452
00:16:46,340 --> 00:16:48,742
- [Servo] Hiya, Mr. B.

453
00:16:48,742 --> 00:16:50,243
- I got a sales report to make.

454
00:16:50,243 --> 00:16:51,678
- Please, Sidney.

455
00:16:51,678 --> 00:16:53,180
It's Mr. Johnson's move.

456
00:16:53,180 --> 00:16:54,748
- [Crow] They're having
a goatee staring contest.

457
00:16:58,685 --> 00:17:01,088
- [Servo] Let's
talk, we never talk.

458
00:17:01,088 --> 00:17:02,188
- I'll have to take your queen.

459
00:17:02,188 --> 00:17:03,390
- How true.

460
00:17:03,390 --> 00:17:04,858
- [Crow] Sorry
about that, chief.

461
00:17:06,927 --> 00:17:07,762
- Check.

462
00:17:08,662 --> 00:17:09,863
And mate.

463
00:17:09,863 --> 00:17:11,732
- [Joel] Hey, you
knocked my block off!

464
00:17:12,833 --> 00:17:14,167
- You win, Mr. Tucker.

465
00:17:14,167 --> 00:17:15,368
- [Servo] Mr. Drucker?

466
00:17:15,368 --> 00:17:17,037
- Twelve minutes.

467
00:17:17,037 --> 00:17:18,672
Perhaps you'll have
better luck next time.

468
00:17:20,040 --> 00:17:21,642
- [Crow] Ah, let's play
Chutes and Ladders next time.

469
00:17:22,542 --> 00:17:23,777
- [Joel] Milk money.

470
00:17:23,777 --> 00:17:26,247
- Don't you ever lose
one of them setoos?

471
00:17:26,247 --> 00:17:27,613
- Rarely, if ever.

472
00:17:27,613 --> 00:17:29,048
- [Servo] Thanks
for asking though.

473
00:17:29,048 --> 00:17:31,518
- And you must be a
virtuous-oso as they say?

474
00:17:31,518 --> 00:17:34,287
- Yes and no, I merely make
sure that my antagonists

475
00:17:34,287 --> 00:17:35,722
are bad chess players.

476
00:17:35,722 --> 00:17:36,790
- [Joel] You're hard to talk to.

477
00:17:36,790 --> 00:17:37,892
- Your report, Sidney.

478
00:17:37,892 --> 00:17:39,192
- Oh, twelve watches.

479
00:17:40,360 --> 00:17:42,162
That comes to an
interesting $325.

480
00:17:43,563 --> 00:17:45,198
- Good hunting.

481
00:17:45,198 --> 00:17:46,967
- [Crow] Boss, why are
you in your pajamas?

482
00:17:46,967 --> 00:17:48,202
- The waitress you
pinched, Sidney.

483
00:17:48,202 --> 00:17:50,970
- Oh, Mr. T, that's
how I express myself.

484
00:17:50,970 --> 00:17:52,472
- [Joel] Express this.

485
00:17:52,472 --> 00:17:54,642
- Quite true, but
this one is new here.

486
00:17:54,642 --> 00:17:56,075
I haven't pinched
her myself yet.

487
00:17:56,075 --> 00:17:57,377
(all groan)

488
00:17:57,377 --> 00:17:59,145
- As you say, Mr. T.

489
00:17:59,145 --> 00:18:00,980
Good hunting.

490
00:18:00,980 --> 00:18:01,948
- Sidney.

491
00:18:01,948 --> 00:18:03,083
- [Joel] Bite me.

492
00:18:03,083 --> 00:18:04,350
- Are you beat?

493
00:18:04,350 --> 00:18:06,520
- Oh, sure man, cool,
way out and long gone,

494
00:18:06,520 --> 00:18:08,055
as they say.

495
00:18:08,055 --> 00:18:09,255
- [Servo] Heh, being sarcastic.

496
00:18:09,255 --> 00:18:11,592
- Fiddlesticks, you want
things and it shows.

497
00:18:11,592 --> 00:18:13,527
- You want things
yourself, Mr. T.

498
00:18:13,527 --> 00:18:15,962
Money, dames, and et cetera.

499
00:18:15,962 --> 00:18:18,365
Why, you own this joint
and you work at it.

500
00:18:18,365 --> 00:18:20,000
You even sprouted a mop.

501
